            <ArticleTitle>Cancer history: A predictor of IPMN subtype and dysplastic status?</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0002-9610(17)31094-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.amjsurg.2017.11.014</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The aim of this study was to determine the association of PMH and FH of pancreatic (PDAC) and non-pancreatic cancers with IPMN malignant risk.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A retrospective review of a prospective database of IPMN patients undergoing resection was performed to assess FH and PMH.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">FH of PDAC was present in 13% of 362 included patients. Of these, 8% had at least one first degree relative (FDR) with PDAC. The rate of PDAC positive FH in non-invasive versus invasive IPMN patients was 14% and 8%, respectively (p = 0.3). In main duct IPMN patients, FH (44%) and PMH of non-pancreatic cancer (16%) was higher than that seen in branch duct IPMN (FH 29%; PMH 6%; p = 0.004 and 0.008).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">FH of PDAC is not associated with IPMN malignant progression. FH and PMH of non-pancreatic cancer is associated with main duct IPMN, the subtype with the highest rate of invasive transformation.</AbstractText>
